<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Muang Sua</h1><p class="st-meta">700 CE – 1353 CE (approx.) · capital Muang Sua</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The early Tai-Lao principality at the meeting of the Mekong and Nam Khan, the site of Luang Prabang and the cradle of the kingdom of Lan Xang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lao chronicles say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khun-lo">Khun Lo</a>, a son of the mythical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khun-borom">Khun Borom</a>, took Muang Sua from its earlier inhabitants and founded a dynasty there, traditionally in the 8th century. The town, also called Xieng Dong Xieng Thong, occupied the site of present-day <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-luang-phrabang">Luang Prabang</a>, which had earlier been settled by Mon-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> peoples.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Muang Sua was a Tai muang, a valley principality ruled by a chief whose authority rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> lands, river trade and ties to other Tai rulers. Lao tradition records a long line of rulers, but few are attested outside the chronicles. In the late 13th century it seems to have come under the influence of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1353 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fa-ngum">Fa Ngum</a>, a Lao prince raised at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> court, captured Muang Sua with Khmer support and made it the capital of his new kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang</a>. The city was renamed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-luang-phrabang">Luang Prabang</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-bang">Phra Bang</a> Buddha image was installed there in the 16th century.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/muang-sua.jpg" alt="Muang Sua" width="320" height="336"><figcaption>Image: Caphtaain · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sukhothai_1293.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 CE – 1353 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Muang Sua</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khun Lo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tai spirit worship, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lao</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Xieng Dong Xieng Thong, Muang Swa, Luang Prabang (early)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1026&amp;focus=muang-sua" aria-label="Muang Sua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7446 1461 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Muang Sua lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7756 1697h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7766 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7766 1717h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Laos. <a href="/atlas?year=1026&amp;focus=muang-sua">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
